Hooks are scripts git runs automatically at specific points, living in `.git/hooks/` (or distributed to every clone via a tool like `pre-commit` or `husky`):

```bash
# .git/hooks/pre-commit — runs before a commit is created
#!/bin/sh
npx eslint --max-warnings=0 $(git diff --cached --name-only) || exit 1

# .git/hooks/commit-msg — runs after the message is written, can reject it
#!/bin/sh
grep -qE '^(feat|fix|chore|docs)(\(.+\))?: ' "$1" || {
  echo "Commit message must follow Conventional Commits format"; exit 1;
}

# .git/hooks/pre-push — runs before objects are sent to the remote
#!/bin/sh
npm test || exit 1
```

Hooks give **local, instant** feedback — a failing lint or a broken convention is caught before it ever reaches CI, saving the round-trip. The trade-off: hooks live in `.git/hooks/` by default, which isn't committed to the repo, so teams need a distribution mechanism (a hooks-manager tool, or a setup script) to make sure everyone actually has them installed — an uninstalled hook enforces nothing.
